**Survey** — 52 tools on 5 servers, 7 read-only probes, 11 papers found.

Warning: two limitations up front. First, my tools were limited to arXiv/web search and metadata, so I did not compute any relic densities. Neither the inelastic-DM tools named in the task nor any relic-density code were available to me. Second, my arXiv searches did not turn up the main recent paper deriving the higgsino thermal mass target (about 1.0-1.2 TeV with Sommerfeld effects). From general knowledge, a pure higgsino (an electroweak doublet) gives the Planck abundance of Omega h^2 of about 0.12 at about 1.1 TeV. Planck 2018 (1807.06209) is the reference for this value; its abstract was cut off, so the exact number should be taken from the paper. Searches did return a pMSSM study of heavy higgsino and wino relic abundance with Sommerfeld enhancement (1411.6930) and general methods for including the Sommerfeld effect in relic calculations (1008.2905, 0910.5221). On direct detection: at tree level a pure higgsino's spin-independent scattering is suppressed. It only reaches xenon detectors through electroweak loops, which give an elastic cross-section well below 1e-46 cm^2 (heavy-WIMP effective theory, 1309.4092). Gaugino mixing adds Higgs exchange; if the mass splitting between the neutral states drops below about 100-200 keV, Z-mediated inelastic scattering switches on and is strongly constrained. The 4.2 tonne-year LZ result (2410.17036), the 3.1 tonne-year XENONnT result (2502.18005) and earlier first runs are the current limits. A 2026 paper (2609.09830) works out loop-corrected elastic and inelastic higgsino scattering rates in LZ. The expected conclusion, still to be confirmed against the published limit curves, is that a thermal pure higgsino near 1.1 TeV sits orders of magnitude below current LZ/XENONnT spin-independent limits, near or under the neutrino fog. Xenon detectors can probe it only if there is enough gaugino mixing or a mass splitting small enough for inelastic scattering.

Quantities worth checking: Omega h^2 against higgsino mass over 0.9-1.3 TeV, with and without the Sommerfeld effect and coannihilation with the charged and second neutral state; find the mass where it crosses about 0.120.; Exact Planck 2018 Omega_c h^2 and its uncertainty (from 1807.06209), and how much the higgsino mass band shifts across that uncertainty.; Charged-neutral mass splitting (about 340 MeV from electroweak loops) and the neutral-neutral splitting as a function of gaugino masses M1 and M2.; Inelastic-scattering cutoff: the largest neutral splitting that xenon can still access (about 100-200 keV for a TeV-mass WIMP at the galactic escape velocity).; Pure-higgsino loop-level elastic cross-section (expected roughly 1e-49 to 1e-48 cm^2; confirm against 1309.4092) compared with LZ 2024 and XENONnT 2025 limits at 1.1 TeV.; How far below the 1.1 TeV LZ limit the higgsino sits (ratio), and its position relative to the neutrino fog..
