Abstract: We re-examine unitarity bounds on the annihilation cross se
[usage]
[tool] d-10 post_finding {"claim": "Running check_all_constraints with is_higgsino=true and delta=350 keV gives Omega h^2 = 0.1045, 0.1083, 0.1202 and 0.1285 at m = 1080, 1100, 1160 and
[finding] f-015 Running check_all_constraints with is_higgsino=true and delta=350 keV gives Omega h^2 = 0.1045, 0.1083, 0.1202 and 0.1285 at m = 1080, 1100, 1160 and 1200 GeV. 
**Finding:** Running check_all_constraints with is_higgsino=true and delta=350 keV gives Omega h^2 = 0.1045, 0.1083, 0.1202 and 0.1285 at m = 1080, 1100, 1160 and 1200 GeV. The tool's relic crossing is therefore at about 1160 GeV, with tension 12.9, 9.8, 0.1 and 7.1 sigma against 0.12 +/- 0.0012. The expected LZ counts are N_LZ = 1.79, 1.97, 2.52 and 2.90, all consistent with the 1 observed event. The Fermi-LAT and CMB checks pass because the inelastic annihilation rate today is only about 5e-42 cm^3/s.
[tool] tc-261022_1-w1-d-10-43 -> ok: Recorded finding f-015.
[tool] d-10 post_finding {"claim": "The splitting dependence at m = 1100 GeV from check_all_constraints is as follows. At delta = 0 (elastic Z exchange, sigma_n = 7.4e-39 cm^2) the tool
[finding] f-016 The splitting dependence at m = 1100 GeV from check_all_constraints is as follows. At delta = 0 (elastic Z exchange, sigma_n = 7.4e-39 cm^2) the tool gives N_LZ
**Finding:** The splitting dependence at m = 1100 GeV from check_all_constraints is as follows. At delta = 0 (elastic Z exchange, sigma_n = 7.4e-39 cm^2) the tool gives N_LZ = 1.1e7, sigma_v today = 2.5e-24 cm^3/s against the Fermi limit of 2.3e-25, and p_ann = 3.4e-28 against the limit of 3.2e-28, so the point is excluded on all three. At delta = 200 keV it gives N_LZ = 5.1e4. At delta = 350 keV it gives N_LZ = 1.97. At delta = 566 keV it gives N_LZ = 0.0, which is kinematically closed. Only a narrow band near delta of about 350 keV reproduces of order 1 LZ event.
