**Completed — Robustness of the tool relic calculation, and the link between gaugino mixing and the splitting. (a) Use compute_relic_density with an explicit sigma_v_cm3_s to find the effective cross section needed for Omega h^2 = 0.120 at 1.1 TeV. Compare it with the tool's built-in higgsino sigma_v and with the Sommerfeld-enhanced literature values. (b) Translate the xenon-accessible splitting window (delta of about 345-385 keV) and the solar bound (566 keV) into gaugino masses M1 and M2, and estimate the mixing at which Higgs-exchange elastic scattering reaches the LZ limit..** (a) Relic cross section (f-026). With is_higgsino=true the tool ignores the sigma_v_cm3_s argument: every call echoed 2.5e-26 x (1100/m)^2 cm^3/s whatever value was passed (tc-68 to tc-71). I therefore varied sigma_v with is_higgsino=false. At m = 1100 GeV, Omega h^2 = 0.1342, 0.1199 and 0.0909 for sigma_v = 2.0, 2.25 and 3.0e-26 cm^3/s (tc-72 to tc-74). Omega scales almost exactly as 1/sigma_v (Omega x sigma_v is about 0.27e-26), so Omega h^2 = 0.120 needs sigma_v = 2.25e-26 cm^3/s at 1.1 TeV. The built-in higgsino value of 2.5e-26 gives 0.1083 at 1100 GeV and reaches 0.120 at 1159 GeV. Under the built-in (1100/m)^2 scaling Omega goes as about m^2, so a 10% change in sigma_v moves the crossing mass by about 5% (about 55-60 GeV). To match the literature masses of 1.08, 1.10 and 1.20 TeV, the built-in rate would need effective factors of about 0.87, 0.90 and 1.07. These are relative to the tool's own normalization, not physical Sommerfeld or coannihilation factors. (b) Gaugino scale for the splitting (f-027). The tree-level formula is delta0 ~ m_Z^2 (s_W^2/M1 + c_W^2/M2) (Nagata and Shirai, arXiv:1410.4549, Eqs. 15 and 34). For M1 = M2 = M it gives M = 2.38e7, 2.25e7, 2.16e7 and 1.47e7 GeV for delta = 350, 370, 385 and 566 keV. The xenon window therefore needs gauginos at about 2.2-2.4 x 10^4 TeV, not the 10-30 TeV suggested in the direction. The solar bound requires M below about 1.5 x 10^4 TeV. With wino exchange only (delta0 = m_W^2/M2) these values are about 22% lower. RG running of the operators and on-shell loop corrections (1410.4549 Eq. 35 and Fig. 6; 2609.09830 Eqs. 19-20) change these by tens of percent, but no exact percentage was extracted. (c) Higgs-exchange elastic scattering (f-028). In the higgsino limit sigma_SI is roughly 5e-46 cm^2 x (1 TeV/M)^2 x (1 + sgn(mu) sin 2beta)^2 (2606.31013 Eqs. 30-33; 1410.4549 Eqs. 46-49). This normalization is my estimate from those formulas, not a quoted value. It reaches the LZ limit of about 3.3e-47 cm^2 at 1.1 TeV at M of about 4 TeV, or about 0.7 TeV with the 1e-45 placeholder. At M of about 4 TeV the splitting is about 2 GeV, so inelastic scattering is closed. In the inelastic window, sigma_SI is about 1e-54 cm^2. The elastic Higgs-exchange signal and the inelastic signal therefore cannot both appear, which answers q-11. Caveats: the literature came through arxiv_agent reading the arXiv HTML pages; cite() was not run, no figures were extracted and no files were written.
